<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gabon</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Libreville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A republic on the Atlantic coast of Central Africa, independent from France in 1960, ruled by the Bongo family for 55 years until a coup in 2023.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a> had been a territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> from 1910. It became an autonomous republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> on 28 November 1958 and fully independent on 17 August 1960. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-mba">Léon M'ba</a> was elected the first president in 1961, with Omar Bongo Ondimba as his vice president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>M'ba suppressed the press and political demonstrations and changed the constitution to vest power in the presidency. In 1964 an army coup against him was ended within 24 hours by French paratroopers who restored him to power. Omar Bongo replaced him in 1967, declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in 1968 under the Parti Démocratique Gabonais, and was re-elected several times. After strikes and demonstrations in 1990, a national conference led to a multi-party constitution in 1991. Bongo won re-election in 1993, 1998 and 2005, and some opponents rejected the results as fraudulent.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Bongo died on 8 June 2009 and his son Ali Bongo was declared the winner of that year's election. In August 2023, after Ali Bongo was announced as winner of a third term, military officers took power, ending the family's 55-year hold, and named General Brice Oligui Nguema transitional leader. A new constitution was approved by referendum in November 2024, and Nguema won the 2025 presidential election with more than 90% of the vote.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gabon.jpg" alt="Gabon" width="330" height="248"><figcaption>Image: Léon Augé and Pierre Claver Eyeghe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Gabon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Libreville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Léon M'ba, Omar Bongo, Ali Bongo, Brice Oligui Nguema</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Roman Catholicism and Protestantism), Islam, traditional indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/gabon">Gabon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gabonese Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=gabon" aria-label="Gabon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4942 2162 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gabon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5262 2418L5262 2418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 2418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Libreville. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=gabon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
